WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.260
Hello, and welcome back to
another episode with ItsDrDan.

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:09.540
And today we're going to be learning how
to count valence electrons and draw Lewis

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.879
structures for atoms, cations, and anions.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.669
So we're looking, we're not going to be
looking at the bonding picture, but just

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.629
how, how do you do the basics, right?

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.430
How to stick to your fundamentals.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.430
So let's just get into it.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.750
So the first thing is, let's say if
I have nitrogen, which if you kind of

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:31.810
remember from your chemistry class,
well, that has an atomic number of seven.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.859
So if you kind of remember,
well, what is an atom?

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:39.120
Atoms are inherently neutral, meaning
they have set, they have the same

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.550
number of protons as they do electrons.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.489
So for, for something like nitrogen,
well, if it has seven protons,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:52.290
being that we have our number here,
then it also has seven electrons.

00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:55.420
And when you were starting that
off, when you first learned about

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.729
electrons, typically we learned how
to organize it via the Bohr model.

00:00:59.189 --> 00:01:03.210
Which if you remember, well, that
first, that first level, that, or that

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.229
shell, it will have two electrons.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.829
And then the next shell is going
to have the other five electrons.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.540
evenly spread out.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.610
So we have 1,2, 3, 4 and 5 all spread out.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.310
Now, what do all of these represent?

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.549
Well, we have our different levels.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:27.000
Well, the ones that we are interested
in today are the outermost level.

00:01:27.459 --> 00:01:30.960
These are referred to as
the valence electrons.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.369
So it's your outer shell.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:38.560
So one way to look at this, this atom is
like, it's like layers of an onion, right?

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.019
You can peel off those individual layers.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.040
Well, what layer do you peel first?

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:43.640
Well, it's the outermost.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.870
So when it comes to cations
and anions, well, that's what's

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:53.510
going to be behaving in all this
chemistry are those outer levels.

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:57.870
Now, you might also have to
label what the inner levels are.

00:01:58.109 --> 00:02:01.920
These are referred to as the
core electrons, which are so

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.600
close to the nucleus, right?

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.930
The center of the atom that typically
they don't behave in chemistry,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.509
in that many chemical reactions.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:11.079
So the valence level is
what we're going to look at.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.410
So we're going to learn some methods
on how to count them, some simple

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.549
ones, so that we just using your
periodic table, what you are given

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.890
on an exam or on a homework for
most of your chemistry classes.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.710
And then we'll go through different
ways to draw Lewis dot structures.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.810
Let's get at some different examples.

00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:42.600
Now, the easiest method for being able
to count valence electrons is by using

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.269
your periodic table and marking the
columns found on the periodic table.

00:02:47.825 --> 00:02:51.715
So if we get, if we look at this and
we look at the first column, what

00:02:51.715 --> 00:02:54.175
we're going to do is we're going
to just label it like how you would

00:02:54.175 --> 00:02:57.714
number any column that you would see,
which would be with the number 1.

00:02:58.215 --> 00:03:01.365
So when we do that, we label
the first one as number one, the

00:03:01.365 --> 00:03:03.225
second column with number two.

00:03:03.875 --> 00:03:07.894
And what those represent is the
number of valence electrons for all

00:03:07.925 --> 00:03:12.855
of the elements in that particular
group or family, or also column.

00:03:13.244 --> 00:03:17.695
So for example, hydrogen, lithium,
and sodium, they all have one valence

00:03:17.695 --> 00:03:20.985
electron in their outermost layer.

00:03:21.274 --> 00:03:21.644
Right.

00:03:22.045 --> 00:03:25.315
So if you're kind of just to show
those as quick examples, right, if

00:03:25.315 --> 00:03:29.475
you think of hydrogen, well, it has
an electron configuration of 1s1,

00:03:29.994 --> 00:03:34.694
meaning that it has one electron in
its outermost level, which is one.

00:03:35.354 --> 00:03:39.474
If you think of lithium, well,
that one is 1s1, or sorry, 1s2.

00:03:40.525 --> 00:03:41.785
And then 2s1.

00:03:42.005 --> 00:03:47.835
Notice once again, we have one
electron in each of those levels.

00:03:48.045 --> 00:03:51.795
And then if we go down to sodium, and
I do the same thing where I write down

00:03:52.415 --> 00:03:58.755
its electron configuration, well, notice
we have the same trend where we have

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:04.175
one electron in its outermost layer,
which is that highest number, right?

00:04:04.175 --> 00:04:06.755
So 1, 2, 3, those are the highest numbers.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:13.140
The second, second column, right, where
Beryllium and Magnesium are, we can also

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:16.190
put Helium in that position as well.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.390
So Helium, even though it is a noble
gas and it's on the far right hand

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:25.899
side of the periodic table, we can
pretend that Helium is located here

00:04:25.969 --> 00:04:31.919
for valence electron purposes, being
that Helium also has two electrons.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:35.770
So then as we kind of go through
that periodic table, right, we can

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.260
fill in the rest of the columns.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:42.000
We can pretty much kind of move this
for now, where we can say like, okay,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.109
let's kind of get rid of helium for
at the moment, where it's going to be

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.130
on that far side, gone away, right?

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:55.170
And we're going to put, okay, so now
that we have those different numbers,

00:04:55.675 --> 00:04:59.805
These represent the number of valence
electrons in their outermost layer.

00:05:00.195 --> 00:05:05.065
Let's practice drawing these as
Lewis structures for many of these

00:05:05.065 --> 00:05:06.715
examples that we have listed above.

00:05:07.014 --> 00:05:10.975
Alright, so our first example of
going through how to draw different

00:05:11.004 --> 00:05:13.455
Lewis dot structures for atoms.

00:05:13.905 --> 00:05:16.375
So let's just go through our
periodic table, practicing

00:05:16.385 --> 00:05:17.835
counting valence electrons.

00:05:18.314 --> 00:05:21.095
So if we start with the first
one, which is hydrogen, right?

00:05:21.105 --> 00:05:24.674
Hydrogen is all the way up here on that
top left side of the periodic table.

00:05:25.055 --> 00:05:27.715
So, and that belongs in
the S orbital, right?

00:05:27.715 --> 00:05:29.715
So that's its highest level.

00:05:30.044 --> 00:05:33.965
So I wrote down all the electron
configurations for these elements

00:05:33.995 --> 00:05:37.255
for us because we just want to
focus on how to write the notation.

00:05:37.845 --> 00:05:42.875
So if I have hydrogen, 1s1, we
can see that, well, that is its

00:05:42.965 --> 00:05:46.635
outermost level is this 1s1 area.

00:05:47.215 --> 00:05:48.875
Now, same thing with Helium, right?

00:05:48.875 --> 00:05:51.414
1s2, that is its highest level.

00:05:51.424 --> 00:05:54.215
So what we're going to do is we're
going to draw the atom first of

00:05:54.294 --> 00:05:58.955
Hydrogen, and we're going to use a
dot to represent its valence electron.

00:05:58.965 --> 00:06:02.594
So being that this one has one
single valence electron, we'll

00:06:02.594 --> 00:06:04.484
just write that as H with a dot.

00:06:04.825 --> 00:06:08.825
And it doesn't matter exactly where you
put that dot, you can put it on the top.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.720
You can put it on the left,
you can put it on the bottom.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.500
They all represent that we have one
valence electron around our hydrogen.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:20.310
And this is going to be important for
when you get into covalent bonding,

00:06:20.389 --> 00:06:24.889
all about how we can make bonds to
share electrons, which will help you

00:06:24.889 --> 00:06:28.499
be able to illustrate that, which
there are other videos on my channel as

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.300
well to help go into covalent bonding.

00:06:31.825 --> 00:06:35.775
Now, with Helium, Helium is the same
idea, so what we're going to be doing

00:06:35.775 --> 00:06:41.035
this time as well, we have a complete
orbital for Helium, meaning we have two

00:06:41.045 --> 00:06:43.515
electrons, it has a complete level, right?

00:06:43.515 --> 00:06:48.435
Remember this, even though I wrote it in
little dashed lines up here, this is like

00:06:48.455 --> 00:06:50.555
a noble, this is a noble guess, right?

00:06:50.655 --> 00:06:55.099
Meaning that it is happy, it's
fulfilling what we refer to as happiness.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:58.370
for this one is the duet rule, right?

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.400
It is, has a complete level.

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:05.780
Um, so for this one, it's going to be
a little different than all the others.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:09.690
We're going to put those two
dots together to help represent.

00:07:10.125 --> 00:07:11.984
that this has a complete orbital.

00:07:12.015 --> 00:07:16.635
And we can put them like I did
before, is on any side, as long as

00:07:16.645 --> 00:07:21.224
you have some kind of consistency
with the way you write these.

00:07:21.885 --> 00:07:23.614
Now, so that's hydrogen and helium, right?

00:07:23.655 --> 00:07:25.125
We have two valence electrons.

00:07:25.595 --> 00:07:26.325
Two dots.

00:07:26.325 --> 00:07:28.505
So we have one valence electron, right?

00:07:28.855 --> 00:07:30.175
Two valence electrons.

00:07:30.245 --> 00:07:33.365
And that's going off the
columns of our periodic table.

00:07:33.365 --> 00:07:35.245
So one and two.

00:07:35.805 --> 00:07:38.344
Um, and just to kind of
help illustrate that, right?

00:07:38.345 --> 00:07:40.045
One, one and two here.

00:07:40.475 --> 00:07:43.374
So our next one, right, is
going to be boron and aluminum.

00:07:43.654 --> 00:07:45.015
So if I have, uh, sorry.

00:07:45.035 --> 00:07:45.875
All right, I go to the next one.

00:07:45.875 --> 00:07:47.705
So if I go down to lithium, right?

00:07:47.705 --> 00:07:48.805
Lithium and beryllium.

00:07:49.385 --> 00:07:52.515
So lithium is right below hydrogen.

00:07:52.855 --> 00:07:54.394
So how many valence
electrons should I have?

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.230
Well, based on our trend here, it
should also have one, but I thought

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:00.610
that lithium has three electrons.

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.100
How is that the case?

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:07.259
Well, what we're going to be doing
is counting its highest level.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.770
So when you are looking at your
periodic table, right, we can see

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:13.300
that lithium is right here, right?

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:15.440
It falls under column number one.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.320
So what we can do is, is by seeing that we
know it has one valence electron, whether

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.649
we have the electron configuration or not.

00:08:24.935 --> 00:08:29.905
One thing to keep in mind is, well, being
that we do, the electron configuration,

00:08:30.025 --> 00:08:35.965
all's we are doing is counting S and
P Electrons in the highest level.

00:08:36.144 --> 00:08:36.694
That is it.

00:08:37.025 --> 00:08:40.215
So we only count s and p, right?

00:08:40.215 --> 00:08:42.895
So I'm going to write that down here
because it's really important to see.

00:08:43.384 --> 00:08:49.295
Only count S and P and
that has to be the highest.

00:08:49.335 --> 00:08:49.925
Okay.

00:08:50.705 --> 00:08:52.505
So, so keep that in mind.

00:08:52.935 --> 00:08:54.554
So that's all we're going to be counting.

00:08:54.885 --> 00:08:59.345
So with lithium, right, what we
can see the highest one is 2S1.

00:08:59.345 --> 00:09:05.235
So once again, we have that
one feature here as our answer.

00:09:05.245 --> 00:09:06.735
So one balanced electron.

00:09:07.325 --> 00:09:11.315
And it is going to also
be just one single dot.

00:09:11.315 --> 00:09:12.875
So let's do this one in a different color.

00:09:13.425 --> 00:09:15.515
So lithium has just one dot.

00:09:15.565 --> 00:09:17.905
And as before, it doesn't
matter where you put it.

00:09:18.794 --> 00:09:19.945
Let's go on to the next.

00:09:20.485 --> 00:09:22.255
Well, the next one, brilliant, right?

00:09:22.305 --> 00:09:25.484
Once again, we can see
our highest level is 2s2.

00:09:26.125 --> 00:09:27.135
How many do we have here?

00:09:27.155 --> 00:09:32.165
Well, now we have two valence
electrons in that highest orbital.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:35.850
So now we can do is
write two dots this time.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:41.230
So we do, um, beryllium
and we write one, two.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:45.309
And notice the difference between
beryllium and helium, right?

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.739
Helium has a complete level.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:49.590
So we write them together.

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:51.809
However, beryllium does not.

00:09:51.819 --> 00:09:54.469
It still has room to grow.

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:58.300
And that's because we now have
access to the, the p electrons.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:04.185
So when you go down to boron, notice how
in the second level, We have p electrons.

00:10:04.665 --> 00:10:09.695
So, we don't have 2 anymore, but we
have 3 valence electrons because we're

00:10:09.695 --> 00:10:11.924
moving down our periodic table, right?

00:10:11.985 --> 00:10:13.734
1, 2, 3.

00:10:14.235 --> 00:10:16.505
The boron now has those three.

00:10:17.165 --> 00:10:21.535
So, and as we keep growing, we can kind
of make some predictions here, right?

00:10:21.575 --> 00:10:24.755
Is that, well, when we go to carbon,
well, that's going to be four valence

00:10:24.755 --> 00:10:27.735
electrons, and we can show this, right?

00:10:28.125 --> 00:10:33.435
The whole idea is that as we grow, we're
going to keep putting dots on every side.

00:10:33.435 --> 00:10:35.605
So, with boron, we'll do one, two, three.

00:10:35.975 --> 00:10:38.495
With carbon, we'll do
one, two, three, four.

00:10:38.879 --> 00:10:43.009
And the way we're drawing these is imagine
that our element is in a little box that

00:10:43.009 --> 00:10:46.009
we're filling the dots around each side.

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:49.650
Now what happens when we
go over, go over four?

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:55.470
Well, if we go to nitrogen, which is now
five valence electrons, and the way to

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:59.759
kind of highlight that, just to keep that
highlighting up, right, so boron was these

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:09.359
two, carbon was, uh, these two here, oops,
and then nitrogen was these five, right?

00:11:09.359 --> 00:11:11.650
So we're adding up the superscripts.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.380
So for nitrogen, two plus three is five.

00:11:15.345 --> 00:11:20.155
So when we are doing this, we are
going to have, write our nitrogen, and

00:11:20.155 --> 00:11:26.235
then we are going to put a dot on each
side, right, 4, and then we're going

00:11:26.235 --> 00:11:28.665
to double up our dots on another side.

00:11:29.109 --> 00:11:31.670
It does not matter where
the double up is happening.

00:11:31.979 --> 00:11:33.349
Personally, I prefer the top.

00:11:33.359 --> 00:11:37.199
That's what most people do when
they are drawing this, right?

00:11:37.199 --> 00:11:39.939
So you can either draw it like
that, or if you want to, you

00:11:39.939 --> 00:11:42.070
can write dot, dot, dot, dot.

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:44.160
And maybe you have them
both on the right hand side.

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:48.840
There is no particular method as
long as you are being consistent.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:51.900
Um, you will hear some
different professors.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:54.689
They'll tell you like, oh, it
has to be top down, left, right.

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:55.679
It does not.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:01.209
Um, with oxygen, all right, same
idea here, right, we're going to go

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:05.089
down that, we're going to highlight
our valence electrons, so 2 plus

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:08.370
4, which means 6 valence electrons.

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:12.189
Notice how that's also matching
up with our column, right, so

00:12:12.199 --> 00:12:13.650
oxygen's here in the 6th one.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:17.970
You see Fluorine is 7, Neon
is 8, so we can keep building

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.130
this, but why don't we do that?

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:28.739
So we'll see that, okay, Oxygen
is 6, Fluorine is 7, and then

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:34.060
8, and then we can write all of
those little pieces for ourselves.

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:40.030
So Oxygen, so Oxygen, we
will do 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

00:12:40.795 --> 00:12:48.135
Fluorine will do 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, and
then Neon, which is our last one, we will

00:12:48.135 --> 00:12:54.304
do 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8, right?

00:12:54.304 --> 00:12:58.334
So we're filling in all those
sides all around our species.

00:12:58.365 --> 00:13:03.604
And this is how we do Lewis dot
structures for just a normal atom.

00:13:03.765 --> 00:13:06.834
Remember, the goal for all
these different elements is to

00:13:06.834 --> 00:13:08.954
get towards our noble gases.

00:13:09.345 --> 00:13:14.959
Right, so either we want to achieve
the octet rule, Or, we want to achieve

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:18.810
the duet rule for hydrogen, helium,
lithium, beryllium around that area.

00:13:18.849 --> 00:13:20.930
We want to be a noble gas.

00:13:21.319 --> 00:13:24.540
So let's look at how to do
this for cations and anions

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:25.680
and look at the difference.

00:13:26.279 --> 00:13:30.279
Alright, so our last example is
talking all about cations and anions.

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:33.870
And if you remember from my
previous videos talking about

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:39.310
ionic species, that was when we
were gaining and losing electrons.

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:44.800
So, to remember that, losing an
electron was all about being a cation,

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:49.810
so meaning when you lose an electron,
you are more positive, so cations

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:54.739
are positive, so if you remember a
cat, right, has paws, it is positive.

00:13:55.255 --> 00:14:00.795
So an anion is negative, think of
that negative thing where onions make

00:14:00.795 --> 00:14:04.745
you cry, that'll help you remember
that onions are more negative,

00:14:05.005 --> 00:14:08.704
meaning more electrons, right, it's
all about a balance of charges.

00:14:09.194 --> 00:14:12.024
So how do we do this when it
comes to Lewis dot structures?

00:14:12.595 --> 00:14:14.225
Let's do our, let's do an easy.

00:14:14.245 --> 00:14:15.175
Let's do a simple one.

00:14:15.685 --> 00:14:22.905
Let's say if I have hydrogen, hydrogen has
one dot, meaning that it has one s one.

00:14:23.415 --> 00:14:27.825
Well, hydrogen, what is the predictable
charge of hydrogen well being?

00:14:27.825 --> 00:14:29.085
It's in that first column.

00:14:29.444 --> 00:14:32.845
It has a predictable
charge of plus one, right?

00:14:33.175 --> 00:14:37.894
So everything in that column would
be a plus one, this is plus two.

00:14:38.244 --> 00:14:40.494
Why don't we just fill in the
rest of our charges as well?

00:14:40.504 --> 00:14:44.554
So let's say if I, I'll do negative
one, negative two, negative three,

00:14:44.644 --> 00:14:46.444
for our predictable charges.

00:14:47.045 --> 00:14:50.435
So hydrogen, it is prone
to giving up an electron.

00:14:50.995 --> 00:14:52.655
So where is that going to come from?

00:14:52.715 --> 00:14:57.184
Well, it's going to come from our
valence level, our outermost shell.

00:14:57.695 --> 00:15:02.855
So when that happens, well, let's
say if we have H and it loses its

00:15:02.865 --> 00:15:08.085
electron, we will put that in brackets
to denote that it lost its electron,

00:15:08.385 --> 00:15:09.835
and then we'll put a positive symbol.

00:15:09.905 --> 00:15:11.985
Notice how it doesn't have a dot anymore.

00:15:12.065 --> 00:15:13.885
It lost its electron.

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:16.010
Now let's try a different one.

00:15:16.369 --> 00:15:21.160
What if instead I do,  we'll
do,  oxygen, for example.

00:15:21.869 --> 00:15:25.209
So if I have oxygen, well,
oxygen has how many dots?

00:15:25.219 --> 00:15:28.319
Well, if we go to our periodic table,
we can double check that for ourselves.

00:15:28.834 --> 00:15:34.444
We can see that oxygen is at six, so
it should have six dots all around it.

00:15:35.185 --> 00:15:40.824
So when we go about and try to write
that for ourselves, we're going

00:15:40.829 --> 00:15:43.105
to fill in all those little dots.

00:15:43.105 --> 00:15:46.944
So we have 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, right?

00:15:46.944 --> 00:15:49.105
So six valence electrons.

00:15:49.954 --> 00:15:54.574
Now if we want to draw,
let's say O two minus, right?

00:15:55.395 --> 00:15:57.255
Well, what is the difference?

00:15:57.275 --> 00:16:01.145
Well, O2- means that it
gained two electrons.

00:16:01.455 --> 00:16:02.765
So it has more electrons.

00:16:02.875 --> 00:16:08.685
This is an anion versus
H which was a cation.

00:16:09.365 --> 00:16:14.004
So when we are an anion, what we now
need to do is not only do we want to

00:16:14.004 --> 00:16:20.785
show the six dots we had before, But we
are going to be adding two dots as well.

00:16:20.805 --> 00:16:22.795
So we'll represent that in orange.

00:16:23.365 --> 00:16:27.124
Now when we have those extra
two dots, notice how the octet

00:16:27.495 --> 00:16:30.445
rule is satisfied, right?

00:16:30.445 --> 00:16:31.945
We have all eight of them.

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:38.150
And we can put here that two negative to
tell you, hey, this is an ion that has

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:42.800
extra electrons that are here, and this
is gonna be really important when you

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:46.580
have to do ionic bonding Lewis structures
and potentially covalent as well.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:50.020
It'll help guide you in seeing
how many bonds these can do.

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:52.130
Let's try another.

00:16:52.670 --> 00:17:00.070
So let's say if I had a sodium,
sodium has one valence electron.

00:17:01.095 --> 00:17:06.200
Now if I want to do sodium
plus, Well, how would I do that?

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.360
Well, it would be just like so.

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:10.520
You'd put it in brackets
and put a plus sign.

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:15.859
Now, depending on where you are learning
chemistry, you can potentially show

00:17:15.860 --> 00:17:18.380
that, um, what is its new valence level.

00:17:18.409 --> 00:17:19.390
Well, let's write it out.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:28.730
So, sodium neutral has
1s2, 2s2, 2p6, 3s1, right?

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:33.310
And we removed, we're going to
be removing this outermost level.

00:17:34.055 --> 00:17:38.535
So sodium plus on the other
hand, well, it just has the same

00:17:38.555 --> 00:17:41.145
configuration as neon, right?

00:17:41.585 --> 00:17:44.795
So when I take this, it has
the following configuration.

00:17:45.265 --> 00:17:49.264
So can you write it where we
have eight valence electrons?

00:17:49.305 --> 00:17:53.575
Well, you can, you can show that,
okay, this has eight valence

00:17:53.585 --> 00:17:56.405
electrons all around it, as long as.

00:17:56.780 --> 00:18:02.130
You illustrate that it has a positive
around it to tell you, Hey, this is

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.050
missing, this missing one electron.

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:09.040
It has this complete octet, so
you can show either or most of

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:14.129
the time though, this is the
preferred and more common method.

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:17.520
However, every class is a little
bit different, which have different

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:19.180
purposes, so keep that in mind.

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:21.889
If you're not sure, you can
always ask your professor or your

00:18:21.889 --> 00:18:23.639
teacher which one they prefer.

00:18:23.780 --> 00:18:28.280
But I know with my students, typically
I'm more in favor of this one, but I

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:29.560
know every class is a little different.

00:18:29.569 --> 00:18:30.629
So keep that in mind, right?

00:18:30.629 --> 00:18:31.190
Little warning.

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:34.199
Anything you see on YouTube,
you want to make sure you double

00:18:34.199 --> 00:18:35.399
check with your professor.

00:18:36.659 --> 00:18:38.979
So that is another example of a cation.

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:40.610
Let's do one more.

00:18:41.149 --> 00:18:44.330
So what if I have, let's say nitrogen?

00:18:44.955 --> 00:18:48.034
Well, nitrogen, let's check how
many valence electrons this has.

00:18:48.075 --> 00:18:49.455
We can go to our periodic table.

00:18:49.875 --> 00:18:54.435
We can see that it is on column
number five here, so it is right

00:18:54.445 --> 00:19:00.114
here, uh, where we see n, n seven,
that tells you seven protons, seven

00:19:00.115 --> 00:19:03.025
electrons, five of which are valence.

00:19:03.785 --> 00:19:06.215
So let's kind of talk
about that for a second.

00:19:06.215 --> 00:19:09.165
So we have seven total electrons.

00:19:09.995 --> 00:19:11.875
Five are valence electrons.

00:19:11.925 --> 00:19:13.125
What about the other two?

00:19:13.254 --> 00:19:14.004
What are they?

00:19:14.334 --> 00:19:18.035
Well, that means that there
are two, four electrons, right?

00:19:18.035 --> 00:19:24.014
So all these added together is going to
be making your total number of electrons.

00:19:24.025 --> 00:19:24.995
So keep that in mind.

00:19:25.495 --> 00:19:30.785
That all the, the core and valence added
together equals your total electrons.

00:19:30.785 --> 00:19:36.965
So sometimes you might have a class that
requires you to  show both those sides.

00:19:37.525 --> 00:19:41.615
So now that we have our nitrogen
here, we're going to write our dots.

00:19:41.615 --> 00:19:42.885
So we had five, right?

00:19:42.885 --> 00:19:45.145
One, two, three, four, five.

00:19:45.895 --> 00:19:48.765
Now, let's try the anion form, right?

00:19:48.765 --> 00:19:54.455
If I tell you N3 minus, well, how
is that going to be changing things?

00:19:54.899 --> 00:20:02.540
Well being that this is one S two two
S, two two P three, the N three minus

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:08.689
is gonna be one SS two, two S two,
and this is going to be two P six.

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:10.400
That's our change, right?

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:15.100
So notice that that number goes
up three because we are an anion.

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:20.110
So when we draw our Lewis dot structure,
we'll write one, two, three, four,

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:25.799
five, and then we'll put our last,
our two, our three new dots as a,

00:20:26.529 --> 00:20:29.139
um, to show that this is an anion.

00:20:29.169 --> 00:20:31.530
And then we would write in
brackets with a three minus.

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:36.480
This is how you can write Lewis dot
structures for atoms, cations, and anions.

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.840
I didn't see a lot of videos like
this on YouTube, and I thought,

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:40.149
let's just go over the basics.

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:45.000
If you would like to go over covalent
and molecular bonded, uh, bonds,

00:20:45.430 --> 00:20:48.920
go to my follow up video on this
on how to draw Lewis structures.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:52.800
It's a little bit of an older video for
now, but hopefully that can help you be

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.270
able to understand these little basics.

